  "transcript": "# The use of Artificial Intelligence and EdTech in Education\n\n## Big picture: opportunities and worries\n> AI is being used across colleges and universities for teaching, admin and support, offering big gains but creating real risks on workload, equity and governance.\n\nChair: We want to hear how AI and edtech are changing further and higher education and how institutions should prepare students for an AI-rich future.\nCath Sezen: It's early days, but colleges are keen: staff use AI to plan lessons and support students, and specialist 'digiTEC' colleges focus on digital and AI skills.\nChris Loveday: AI helps teaching, admin and operations. It can reduce workload if used well, but staff and students need training and safeguards on misinfo, bias and data use.\nMary Meekings: UCU members' experiences vary widely—some embrace AI, some worry. Staff are teaching themselves and sometimes paying for tools; that can't be sustainable.\nBen Rowland: Providers report fastest gains in back-office automation, then teaching prep; learning and assessment improvements lag. Small, practical providers experiment quickly with supplier help.\n\n\n## Curriculum, skills and digital fairness\n> Everyone will need basic digital and critical-thinking skills, but colleges fear the digital divide will leave many behind without proper devices, connectivity and training.\n\nJodie Gosling: How will AI affect new post-16 reforms like V-levels and T-levels?\nCath Sezen: Digital content is being added to T-levels and V-levels, but qualifications move slowly; we must speed that up while keeping higher technical pathways available.\nBen Rowland: Simulation and VR now make realistic practice affordable—useful for vocational training and for building confidence in maths and English resits.\nChris Loveday: Curriculum must teach how to use AI, and institutions must protect students who lack devices, internet or paid-model access—there are four equity pillars to cover.\nMary Meekings: We risk a digital underclass or, conversely, students relying too much on machines. The human relationship in education must be preserved.\nManuela Perteghella: Could AI help reduce the number of young people who are NEET?\nBen Rowland: AI can support outreach and retraining, but social factors and employer engagement matter; apprenticeships—including older workers—are crucial routes in.\nChris Loveday: Using live data to identify at-risk students earlier helps target support and prevent disengagement; tech should guide strategy, not replace it.\n\n\n## Assessment, integrity and what we measure\n> AI forces a rethink of assessment: practical, in-person checks, or AI-aware tasks, plus oral or process-based evidence, will be needed to certify learning.\n\nDarren Paffey: How should assessment change now that students can use generative AI?\nChris Loveday: We should reassess softer skills like critical thinking and presentations and use timed, supervised tasks where appropriate; marking tells you about students too.\nCath Sezen: Vocational assessment should focus on doing the task, not just written recall. This moment may justify a wider rethink of assessment design.\nBen Rowland: Surveys show assessment is the area least affected so far. Awarding bodies want to innovate but feel regulators like Ofqual are not aligned with frontline needs.\nManuela Perteghella: Are dissertations and postgraduate research at risk from AI?\nProfessor Simon Banks: Research and dissertations can still include oral defences and ongoing supervision; the discipline and how central writing or coding are will determine risk.\nCharlotte Armstrong: Students report mixed effects: many use AI to summarise or plan rather than to submit unedited essays, but some feel guilty and worry they are learning less.\nMichael Webb: Technical approaches include supervised assessments, process monitoring that records how work was produced, and oral checks—no single fix will work as tools evolve.\n\n\n## Staffing, workload and governance\n> AI can reduce tedious tasks but currently often shifts workload into checking and adjusting outputs; colleges lack consistent governance and need funded training.\n\nJodie Gosling: Will AI cut staff numbers or just change staff skills?\nCharlotte Armstrong: Staff will need time for training. The best evidence shows AI plus human feedback works well—losing staff-student interaction would harm quality.\nProfessor Simon Banks: I expect upskilling rather than mass reductions. Many staff use AI to do new things, and time saved often gets reinvested into teaching in different ways.\nChris Loveday: Governance is the biggest risk. Many colleges lack clear AI policies, data protection officers or guidance, so staff may accidentally share sensitive data with free tools.\nBen Rowland: Larger providers have governance driven by scale or private owners; small providers struggle. Claiming extra learner-support funds is also administratively hard.\nMary Meekings: Workload surveys show workloads rising year-on-year. AI hasn't yet produced a clear sector-wide workload reduction—teachers need time, training and guardrails.\nDarren Paffey: Who should decide if a product is safe: suppliers, colleges or government?\nChris Loveday: Suppliers sell products and will lock customers in. Responsibility primarily sits with institutions, but colleges need sector-level guidance and support.\nCath Sezen: Colleges want road maps and baseline guidance. We should agree guardrails and avoid multiple bodies duplicating guidance—coordination between Jisc, Ufi and sector bodies helps.\n\n\n## Higher education: research, equity and a national framework\n> Universities are already experimenting; research gains look promising, but students and staff face uneven support—principles-led national guidance and funding for training are needed.\n\nChair: Turning to higher education—how is student AI use affecting deep learning and critical thinking?\nLewis Wilson: Students use AI for many reasons, including lack of staff time. It doesn't always mean they learn less, but shortcuts risk long-term skills erosion if used in place of study.\nProfessor Simon Banks: Impact varies by student and by support. With training, AI can amplify learning; without it, students may 'offload' vital cognitive work.\nCharlotte Armstrong: HEPI surveys show about half of students say AI improves their experience, 16% say it worsens it; those who feel worse often worry they are learning less despite getting grades.\nPeter Swallow: Are universities equipping students with the AI and critical-literacy skills employers want?\nMichael Webb: Universities want to prepare students, but delivery varies. Infrastructure, funding and staff capacity affect how well lessons reach students.\nCharlotte Armstrong: Students in arts and humanities feel less well equipped than STEM students; discipline and institution make a big difference.\nProfessor Simon Banks: Universities must embed AI skills in disciplines while keeping core knowledge and transferable skills—communication, judgement and teamwork—at the heart of degrees.\nDarren Paffey: Should there be a national framework for AI use in universities?\nMichael Webb: Universities need autonomy; a rigid national rule would either be too vague or too prescriptive. Sector-level principles and guidance work better.\nCharlotte Armstrong: Yes to guidance on values and principles rather than hard rules—something like Ireland's higher-education authority approach, not central diktat.\nLewis Wilson: Any framework must be resourced properly so institutions can implement it and no students are left behind.\nChair: Thank you all. Please send any further evidence or detail to the Committee—your points on governance, assessment redesign and funding were especially helpful.\n",
